<p style="text-align: center"><strong>What happens when people encounter misinformation?</strong></p>
<p>Although people have <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">31</strong><select data-qnum="31" name="question_31" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 31 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. constant conflict">A. constant conflict</option><option value="B. additional evidence">B. additional evidence</option><option value="C. different locations">C. different locations</option><option value="D. experimental subjects">D. experimental subjects</option><option value="E. short period">E. short period</option><option value="F. extreme distrust">F. extreme distrust</option><option value="G. frequent exposure">G. frequent exposure</option><option value="H. mental operation">H. mental operation</option><option value="I. dubious reason">I. dubious reason</option><option value="J. different ideas">J. different ideas</option></select></span></span> to misinformation, there is debate about precisely how and when we label something as true or untrue. The philosophers Descartes and Spinoza had <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">32</strong><select data-qnum="32" name="question_32" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 32 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. constant conflict">A. constant conflict</option><option value="B. additional evidence">B. additional evidence</option><option value="C. different locations">C. different locations</option><option value="D. experimental subjects">D. experimental subjects</option><option value="E. short period">E. short period</option><option value="F. extreme distrust">F. extreme distrust</option><option value="G. frequent exposure">G. frequent exposure</option><option value="H. mental operation">H. mental operation</option><option value="I. dubious reason">I. dubious reason</option><option value="J. different ideas">J. different ideas</option></select></span></span> about how people engage with information. While Descartes believed that people accept or reject information after considering whether it is true or not, Spinoza argued that people accepted all information they encountered (and by default misinformation) and did not verify or reject it until afterwards. Moreover, Spinoza believed that a distinct <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">33</strong><select data-qnum="33" name="question_33" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 33 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. constant conflict">A. constant conflict</option><option value="B. additional evidence">B. additional evidence</option><option value="C. different locations">C. different locations</option><option value="D. experimental subjects">D. experimental subjects</option><option value="E. short period">E. short period</option><option value="F. extreme distrust">F. extreme distrust</option><option value="G. frequent exposure">G. frequent exposure</option><option value="H. mental operation">H. mental operation</option><option value="I. dubious reason">I. dubious reason</option><option value="J. different ideas">J. different ideas</option></select></span></span> is involved in these stages. Recent research has provided <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">34</strong><select data-qnum="34" name="question_34" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 34 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. constant conflict">A. constant conflict</option><option value="B. additional evidence">B. additional evidence</option><option value="C. different locations">C. different locations</option><option value="D. experimental subjects">D. experimental subjects</option><option value="E. short period">E. short period</option><option value="F. extreme distrust">F. extreme distrust</option><option value="G. frequent exposure">G. frequent exposure</option><option value="H. mental operation">H. mental operation</option><option value="I. dubious reason">I. dubious reason</option><option value="J. different ideas">J. different ideas</option></select></span></span> for Spinoza’s theory and it would appear that people accept all encountered information as if it were true, even if this is for an extremely <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">35</strong><select data-qnum="35" name="question_35" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 35 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. constant conflict">A. constant conflict</option><option value="B. additional evidence">B. additional evidence</option><option value="C. different locations">C. different locations</option><option value="D. experimental subjects">D. experimental subjects</option><option value="E. short period">E. short period</option><option value="F. extreme distrust">F. extreme distrust</option><option value="G. frequent exposure">G. frequent exposure</option><option value="H. mental operation">H. mental operation</option><option value="I. dubious reason">I. dubious reason</option><option value="J. different ideas">J. different ideas</option></select></span></span>, and do not label the information as true or false until later. This is consistent with the fact that the resources for scepticism and the resources for perceiving and encoding are in <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">36</strong><select data-qnum="36" name="question_36" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 36 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. constant conflict">A. constant conflict</option><option value="B. additional evidence">B. additional evidence</option><option value="C. different locations">C. different locations</option><option value="D. experimental subjects">D. experimental subjects</option><option value="E. short period">E. short period</option><option value="F. extreme distrust">F. extreme distrust</option><option value="G. frequent exposure">G. frequent exposure</option><option value="H. mental operation">H. mental operation</option><option value="I. dubious reason">I. dubious reason</option><option value="J. different ideas">J. different ideas</option></select></span></span> in the brain.</p>                                                <div class="options-dnd-panel dnd-panel dnd-panel--inline" data-dnd-group="137441">
